---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import HeroSecondary from '@/components/HeroSecondary.astro';
import { getLocalizedUrl } from '@/i18n/helpers';

const locale = 'en';
const githubUrl = 'https://github.com/melafrit/ai-strategy';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About AI Strategy — team and editorial approach"
  description="The team, author and philosophy behind AI Strategy: an independent, trilingual pedagogical site for executives, consultants and educators."
>
  <HeroSecondary
    surtitle="About"
    title="The team and the project's philosophy"
    lead="This site is run by a single person. It assumes its subjectivity, its Mediterranean point of view, and its ambition: to make AI strategy readable and operable in French as well as in English and Arabic."
  />

  <article class="ai-container ai-prose ai-about-page">
    <section>
      <h2>The author</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Mohamed El Afrit</strong> is an <strong>educator, trainer and consultant</strong> in
        software engineering, data science, and project and information systems management. This
        site is his response to a recurring observation in engagements and in class:
      </p>
      <blockquote>
        Good content on AI for managers exists, but it is scattered, English-only, paywalled, or
        wrapped in consulting firm rhetoric. French-speakers and Arabic-speakers deserve a
        structured, sourced, and free entry point.
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        The site is his personal contribution to filling this gap. It is not financed by any
        sponsor. No firm, school, or organization sponsors the project — this is an editorial
        choice to preserve the independence of recommendations.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Why the MIT Sloan program as reference</h2>
      <p>
        The program <em>Artificial Intelligence: Implications for Business Strategy</em> offered
        by MIT Sloan School of Management and MIT Computer Science and Artificial Intelligence
        Laboratory has two rare qualities:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          It is <strong>resolutely managerial and strategic</strong>, not technical. It addresses
          executives and managers, not future data scientists.
        </li>
        <li>
          It is <strong>structured and complete</strong>: six modules covering AI, machine
          learning, generative AI, robotics, governance, and the construction of a roadmap.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        That makes it an excellent <strong>reference for inspiration</strong> when structuring a
        French-speaking pedagogical site. This site is not a copy of that program — it is an
        independent pedagogical resource that shares its spirit and rigor, but develops its own
        content in French, English and Arabic, with its own cases, its own pedagogical choices,
        and its own assumed limitations (see
        <a href={getLocalizedUrl('/methode/', locale)}>Method</a>).
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Why three languages</h2>
      <p>
        AI is not an English-only topic. The strategic, ethical, and economic stakes of AI arise
        everywhere, and quality content in each language rarely lacks by chance — it lacks because
        no one has yet taken the time to produce it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The site is trilingual by commitment:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>French</strong> — main language of the project, language of writing, and original
          language of the author. Phase 4 produces French first.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>English</strong> — for international portability and accessibility to a wider
          audience. Translation scheduled in Phase 7.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Arabic</strong> — because executives, consultants and students from the Maghreb
          and the Middle East deserve a worthy entry point. Editorial parity FR/AR is a commitment,
          not an option. Translation scheduled in Phase 7.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>A simple philosophy</h2>
      <p>
        Three editorial principles structure every line of the site:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>No invention.</strong> No fabricated cases, no manufactured statistics, no
          imaginary quotes. Every factual claim has a verifiable source, flagged by the
          <code>&lt;SourceTag /&gt;</code> component.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No jargon.</strong> Technical concepts are explained when they are necessary
          for managerial decision-making. The rest — implementation details — has no place here.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No marketing.</strong> No excessive promises, no unexplained buzzwords, no
          "transform your business with AI in 30 days". AI is a serious topic that deserves a
          serious voice.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        These principles are recorded in the
        <a href={`${githubUrl}/blob/main/docs/cadrage/charte-de-cadrage.md`} rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">
          project framing charter
        </a>
        and in the <a href={getLocalizedUrl('/methode/', locale)}>Method page</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The project is open</h2>
      <p>
        The code and content are public on
        <a href={githubUrl} rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">GitHub</a>
        under dual licensing (see <a href={getLocalizedUrl('/methode/', locale)}>Method</a>).
        Any correction, suggestion or contribution is welcome via
        <a href={`${githubUrl}/issues`} rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">GitHub Issues</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you are an educator adopting the site in a course, a consultant using it in an
        engagement, or an executive deriving a deliverable from it — a notification by email or
        via an issue is appreciated, if only to measure the project's usefulness.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        The official channel is
        <a href={`${githubUrl}/issues/new`} rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">GitHub Issues</a>.
        This allows:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Keeping a public trace of exchanges for the benefit of other readers.</li>
        <li>Avoiding the noise of a saturated mailbox.</li>
        <li>Prioritizing factual corrections over other requests.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        For a sensitive request (for example, a case where you are an actor in a cited
        organization), you can open a private issue simply mentioning the need for discretion.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .ai-about-page { padding-block: var(--ai-space-7) var(--ai-space-8); }
  .ai-about-page section { margin-block: var(--ai-space-7); }
  .ai-about-page section:first-of-type { margin-block-start: 0; }
  .ai-about-page blockquote {
    border-inline-start: 3px solid var(--ai-accent-primary);
    padding-inline-start: var(--ai-space-4);
    margin: var(--ai-space-5) 0;
    color: var(--ai-fg-secondary);
    font-style: italic;
  }
</style>
